\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{ViT token pruning.}
DynamicViT~\cite{rao2021dynamicvit}, EViT~\cite{liang2022evit}, A-ViT~\cite{yin2022avit}, and Token Merging~\cite{bolya2023tome} remove 50--66\% of tokens with under 0.5\,pp ImageNet accuracy loss, but robotic manipulation inverts the spatial-insensitivity assumption. None has been evaluated in closed-loop VLA control.

\paragraph{Token efficiency in VLMs and VLAs.}
FastV~\cite{chen2024fastv} and PruMerge~\cite{shang2024prumerge} reduce vision tokens in VLMs using attention signals. VLA-specific methods have emerged: VLA-IAP~\cite{cheng2026vlaiap}, VLA-Cache~\cite{xu2025vlacache}, EfficientVLA~\cite{yang2025efficientvla}, VLA-Pruner~\cite{liu2025vlapruner}, LightVLA~\cite{jiang2026lightvla}, and Compressor-VLA~\cite{gao2025compressorvla}. Each validates on a single architecture; we ask how architectural choices determine which compression approaches succeed or fail.

\paragraph{Vision connectors.}
LLaVA~\cite{liu2023llava} projects patches independently through an MLP; BLIP-2~\cite{li2023blip2} uses cross-attention queries; pixel shuffle~\cite{shi2016subpixel} folds spatial neighborhoods into channels. These create qualitatively different token dependencies whose pruning consequences have not been characterized. We provide the first cross-architecture analysis of how connector coupling and task complexity jointly govern VLA token compressibility.

